**Ars Poetica (Updated Terms of Service)**

If you would craft a verse, first clear your cache;  
let the algorithm feed you lines you can’t erase.  
Don’t chase the Muse through meadows—she’s behind a paywall;  
subscribe to inspiration, then claim you’re the author.

Make every stanza scrollable, every image viral;  
if it lacks a caption, it’s not worth the scroll.  
Forget the golden mean—go loud, go niche, go branded;  
the reader is a metric, the heart is a brand.

A poem is like a picture—unless it’s a meme;  
if it can’t be cropped, it’s not fit for the feed.  
Use iambic pentameter? No, use bullet points;  
the meter is the engagement, the rhyme is the point.

Be polished, be authentic—steal with confidence;  
if Horace read your draft, he’d call it “content.”  
Keep your ending open, your thesis ambiguous;  
mystery converts better than a moral, dear.

And when the critics praise you, don’t take it personally;  
they’re just practicing their art of being mean.  
For poetry is not a song, but a portfolio piece:  
post it, monetize it, and call it complete.
